WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.325
What the vendors say, at the time of writing.

2
00:00:02.605 --> 00:00:07.491
These are fast-moving facts, checked on November 11, 2026.

3
00:00:07.771 --> 00:00:10.587
Recheck the linked pages before relying on them.

4
00:00:10.867 --> 00:00:11.827
Claude Code.

5
00:00:12.107 --> 00:00:18.805
The model configuration docs say the default on most plans and on the Anthropic API is Opus 5.

6
00:00:19.085 --> 00:00:20.664
5, at medium effort.

7
00:00:20.944 --> 00:00:34.640
The best-practices page says the default Opus handles most coding well, Sonnet costs less on routine work, and Haiku is a good fit for subagents doing simple jobs such as collecting the files that import a module.

8
00:00:34.920 --> 00:00:44.520
The fable alias selects the Fable model, described as being for the hardest, longest-running tasks; it is never the default and can bill to usage credits.

9
00:00:44.800 --> 00:00:49.536
There’s also opusplan, which uses Opus while planning and Sonnet while executing.

10
00:00:49.816 --> 00:00:51.416
Effort, per the same docs.

11
00:00:51.696 --> 00:00:56.176
low for quick exchanges you review closely (brainstorming, a small rename).

12
00:00:56.456 --> 00:00:58.909
medium for day-to-day work with a clear scope.

13
00:00:59.189 --> 00:01:11.136
high or xhigh where getting it right depends on cases that are easy to overlook: a bug in code you didn’t write, a flaky test, a schema change that must find every reader of the old shape.

14
00:01:11.416 --> 00:01:17.859
max for hard problems you want worked through without you, with a warning that it tends to overthink ordinary tasks.

15
00:01:18.139 --> 00:01:26.480
The docs also say phrases like “think hard” are ordinary text, not switches; ultrathink requests deeper reasoning for one turn.

16
00:01:26.760 --> 00:01:28.253
OpenAI Codex.

17
00:01:28.533 --> 00:01:46.709
The Codex models page lists a most-capable model, a lower-cost model with near-top performance aimed at repeated, long-running work, and efficient models for focused, high-volume tasks, plus reasoning-effort settings from light to max (and an “ultra” level that splits work across subagents).

18
00:01:46.989 --> 00:01:47.971
xAI.

19
00:01:48.251 --> 00:01:53.925
The xAI models page, at the time of writing, recommends its flagship Grok model for code.

20
00:01:54.205 --> 00:02:02.909
Notice the pattern: every vendor recommends its strongest model as the default for coding and a smaller one for narrow, repetitive subtasks.

21
00:02:03.189 --> 00:02:04.768
That’s a reasonable prior.

22
00:02:05.048 --> 00:02:07.096
It is not evidence about your tasks.
